1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:05,188
Figure eight. Issue to draft PR.

2
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:27,540
Now the agent writes real code. fix-issue takes a GitHub issue to a draft pull request. Before its gate, it reads and thinks. After it, it writes: a branch, commits, a push, a pull request. The gate sits on that seam.

3
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:33,916
The checkout is clean, on main. Hand it the issue number.

4
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:45,836
It fetched the issue, called it a feature, wrote a plan, and checked that every acceptance criterion maps to a step. Then it stopped.

5
00:00:48,757 --> 00:00:57,459
The plan even keeps tide times in UTC, the convention from the project notebook. Approve, and only now does any code get written.

6
00:00:59,183 --> 00:01:18,491
Your checkout stays safe because the run gets its own git worktree: a second working tree of the repo, on a branch named for the run. Every node runs there. It implements, runs the project's own checks, fixes what fails, and a hard gate refuses to open a PR over broken checks.

7
00:01:19,491 --> 00:01:25,071
Checks pass. It pushes the branch, opens the pull request, and makes sure it's a draft.

8
00:01:27,591 --> 00:01:37,315
Three reviewers then read the diff for correctness, conventions, and coverage, and a judge keeps only what it can confirm. Nothing needed fixing.

9
00:01:38,791 --> 00:01:42,643
There's no CI on this sandbox, so the PR stays a draft.

10
00:01:42,891 --> 00:01:45,615
Your checkout is still main, still clean.

11
00:01:48,291 --> 00:01:50,503
And the draft is waiting on GitHub.

12
00:01:53,071 --> 00:02:02,971
So the agent can't write before you approve, can't touch your checkout, and can't open a PR over failing checks. You review a draft, not a surprise.

13
00:02:03,571 --> 00:02:07,031
Read, approve, then write, somewhere else.

14
00:02:09,731 --> 00:02:12,999
Keelson. Lay the keel, raise the ribs.

